--TEST--
a `secret`-qualified class constant written into an attribute payload is refused where it is written, in both attach forms, nested inside a payload's containers, at every attach site and through an inherited declaration — `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s attribute-payload sink, `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`'s fifth sink exists *because* of `rule:attributes/payload-is-a-compile-time-constant` rather than in
// spite of it: a payload admits only compile-time constants, and a class
// constant is one of the shapes it admits, so the storage class a `secret`
// value can live in is the only way one could reach a payload at all. Every
// other spelling of a secret — a variable, a call — is already refused by
// § 2 for being computed.
type Meta = {label: mixed};

class Keys {
    public const string PLAIN = "/x";
    public const tainted string TAINTED = "t";
    public const secret string TOKEN = "abc";
    public const secret bytes BLOB = "b";
    public const secret tainted string BOTH = "c";
}

// The inherited half: `rule:security/secret-qualifier`'s qualifier belongs to the declaration, so
// reaching it through a subclass is the same constant and the same refusal.
class Derived extends Keys {
}

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. A plain constant is metadata like any
// other, and `tainted` is deliberately accepted: `rule:security/tainted-qualifier` answers "is this
// value's shape safe for a sink", which is a different question from "is this
// value confidential", and no ADR names a payload as a taint sink.
#[{route: Keys::PLAIN}]
#[Meta(label: Keys::TAINTED)]
class Accepted {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms`'s bare attach form, which names no shape to satisfy at all —
// so the refusal is about the value and never about the type it failed.
#[{token: Keys::TOKEN}]
class BareForm {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// § 1's named form, whose shape the payload *is* checked against. The `bytes`
// base is the qualifier's other one.
#[Meta(label: Keys::BLOB)]
class NamedForm {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// A payload's containers are folded into the same constant pool as its top
// level, so the walk asks at every value it reaches rather than only at the
// fields. Both bits composed, which never launder each other.
#[{keys: [Keys::BOTH]}]
class InAnArray {
    public int $n = 1;
}

#[{inner: {nested: Keys::TOKEN}}]
class InAnObjectLiteral {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// The inherited declaration, reached through the subclass that declares none
// of its own.
#[{token: Derived::TOKEN}]
class Inherited {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// The member attach sites `rule:attributes/attach-sites-and-forms` gives, each of which reaches the same
// walk: a property's, a method's and a parameter's.
class EveryAttachSite {
    #[{token: Keys::TOKEN}]
    public int $n = 1;

    #[{token: Keys::TOKEN}]
    public function run(#[{token: Keys::TOKEN}] int $x): int {
        return $x;
    }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0727]: a `secret`-qualified class constant cannot appear in an attribute payload; `Keys::TOKEN` would be folded into the compiled unit's constant pool and handed back to anything that reads the attribute
   --> case.nvs:36:11
